<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>Power Electronics</h1>
<article lang="en">
<header><h2>Rectifiers: Diode and Thyristor AC-DC Conversion</h2><p>The Rectifier studio converts a sinusoidal supply into a unidirectional load voltage and reports the quantities that define converter quality: average output, RMS output, form factor and ripple factor.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and meaning</h3><p>This tool simulates single-phase and three-phase rectifiers, from uncontrolled diode circuits (firing angle <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha = 0^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>) to phase-controlled thyristor (SCR) converters. It draws the source, the output voltage and the load current on the scope for resistive, inductive, capacitive and back-EMF loads, with or without a freewheeling diode, and computes <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, power factor and efficiency from the sampled waveforms.</p></section>
<section><h3>Mathematical model</h3>
<h4>Average output voltage</h4><p>For a single-phase fully-controlled bridge with continuous conduction (a highly inductive load that keeps the current non-zero), the average output is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{2V_m}{\pi}\cos\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Setting <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha = 0^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives the diode bridge value <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2V_m}{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha &gt; 90^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span> negative, which is inverter-mode operation when the load supplies energy (for example a back-EMF source). A resistive load enters discontinuous conduction and gives <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{V_m}{\pi}(1 + \cos\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> instead.</p>
<h4>RMS value, form factor and ripple factor</h4><p>With continuous conduction the output waveform is a rectified, shifted sine whose RMS value stays <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms} = \frac{V_m}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for every <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so only the average moves. The form factor is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF = V_{rms}/V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the ripple factor <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF = \sqrt{(V_{rms}/V_{dc})^2 - 1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> measures the AC content relative to the DC content. Both grow quickly as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> approaches <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> because <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span> shrinks while <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms}</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not.</p>
<h4>Three-phase bridge</h4><p>The six-pulse bridge with peak phase voltage <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{3\sqrt{3}\,V_m}{\pi}\cos\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> under continuous conduction, and the output ripple frequency rises to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6f</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The higher pulse number gives a much lower <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF</annotation></semantics></math></span> than the single-phase circuit and needs far less filtering.</p>
<h4>Freewheeling diode and commutation</h4><p>With an inductive load, the output voltage would go negative once the supply reverses. A freewheeling diode clamps the output to zero at that moment and carries the load current, so the thyristors turn off naturally and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span> follows <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{V_m}{\pi}(1 + \cos\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Source inductance <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_s</annotation></semantics></math></span> delays current transfer between devices by an overlap angle <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>, described by <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\alpha - \cos(\alpha + \mu) = \frac{2\omega L_s I_d}{V_m}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and removes the average voltage <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta V_{dc} = \frac{2\omega L_s}{\pi} I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
<h4>Governing equations</h4>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{2 V_m}{\pi}\cos\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Average DC output of the single-phase fully-controlled bridge, continuous conduction.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms} = \frac{V_m}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>RMS output voltage under continuous conduction, independent of the firing angle.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF = \frac{V_{rms}}{V_{dc}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Form factor: ratio of RMS to average output voltage.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF = \sqrt{\left(\frac{V_{rms}}{V_{dc}}\right)^2 - 1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Ripple factor derived from the form factor.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{V_m}{\pi}\left(1 + \cos\alpha\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Average output with a freewheeling diode or a resistive load.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{3\sqrt{3}\,V_{m}}{\pi}\cos\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Average output of the three-phase six-pulse controlled bridge.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\alpha - \cos(\alpha + \mu) = \frac{2\,\omega L_s I_d}{V_m}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Commutation overlap angle caused by the source inductance.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta V_{dc} = \frac{2\,\omega L_s}{\pi}\, I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Average voltage drop due to commutation overlap.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section><h3>Canvas parameter and control mapping</h3><dl>
<dt>Phase (1-Φ / 3-Φ)</dt><dd>Selects between the single-phase relations with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2V_m/\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the three-phase relation with <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\sqrt{3}V_m/\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and sets the pulse number of the output.</dd>
<dt>Rectifier Type and Full-Wave Subtype</dt><dd>Half-wave, full-wave bridge or center-tapped. Half-wave has a single pulse per cycle, so its average is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_m/\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha = 0^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the full-wave circuits double the pulse count.</dd>
<dt>Voltage Format and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt><dd>The input is read either as an RMS value or as the peak <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Internally <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_m = \sqrt{2}\,V_{RMS}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is used in every formula.</dd>
<dt><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math></span> [Hz]</dt><dd>The supply frequency sets <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega = 2\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span>, shown in the panel, and the period of the scope. It also scales the overlap term <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega L_s</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> slider (<span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>)</dt><dd>The thyristor firing delay. At <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha = 0^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> the circuit behaves as a diode rectifier; the metric <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{DC}</annotation></semantics></math></span> follows <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Load Type (R, RL, RC, RLC, RLE)</dt><dd>Chooses which of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> are active. Inductance gives continuous current, capacitance gives a peak-charging waveform with large ripple, and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> creates a conduction blocking threshold.</dd>
<dt>Free-Wheeling Diode</dt><dd>Available for inductive loads. Switching it on clamps negative output segments and replaces the continuous-conduction formula with the <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1 + \cos\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></span> form.</dd>
<dt>Metrics panel</dt><dd>Displays <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{RMS}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{DC}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{RMS}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{DC}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{DC}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{AC}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{ripple}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PF</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, all computed from the waveform samples.</dd>
</dl></section>
<section><h3>Worked numerical example</h3><h4>Single-phase controlled bridge, highly inductive load</h4><p>A single-phase fully-controlled bridge is fed with a peak voltage <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_m = 100\text{ V}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, fires at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha = 60^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and supplies a load of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R = 10\text{ }\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> whose inductance keeps the current continuous. Find the output quality figures.</p><ol>
<li><p>Compute the average output voltage from the continuous-conduction formula.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>63.66</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>31.83</mn><mtext> V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = \frac{2 \cdot 100}{\pi}\cos 60^\circ = 63.66 \times 0.5 = 31.83\text{ V}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The RMS value does not depend on <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> in continuous conduction.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>70.71</mn><mtext> V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms} = \frac{100}{\sqrt{2}} = 70.71\text{ V}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Form factor from the two voltages.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>70.71</mn><mn>31.83</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2.221</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF = \frac{70.71}{31.83} = 2.221</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Ripple factor from the form factor.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>2.221</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3.935</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1.984</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF = \sqrt{2.221^2 - 1} = \sqrt{3.935} = 1.984</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>Average load current and DC power, using <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{dc} = V_{dc}/R</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>31.83</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3.183</mn><mtext> A</mtext><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>31.83</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3.183</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>101.3</mn><mtext> W</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{dc} = \frac{31.83}{10} = 3.183\text{ A}, \qquad P_{dc} = 31.83 \times 3.183 = 101.3\text{ W}</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
</ol><p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>31.83</mn><mtext> V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc} = 31.83\text{ V}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>70.71</mn><mtext> V</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{rms} = 70.71\text{ V}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.221</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FF = 2.221</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.984</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RF = 1.984</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Raising <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></span> toward <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> drives <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{dc}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to zero and the ripple factor to infinity, which is why the converter is used with a filter or a freewheeling path.</p></section>
<section><h3>Industrial applications and standards</h3><dl>
<dt>DC motor drives</dt><dd>Phase-controlled bridges regulate armature voltage in rolling mills, cranes and machine-tool spindles, and inverter mode allows regenerative braking.</dd>
<dt>Battery charging and electrolysis</dt><dd>Controlled rectifiers supply adjustable DC to chargers, electroplating and electrochemical plants, where the average current must be tuned precisely.</dd>
<dt>HVDC and renewable grids</dt><dd>Large three-phase thyristor bridges convert at transmission level, with commutation overlap setting the voltage regulation of the link.</dd>
<dt>Welding and industrial heating</dt><dd>Phase control sets the output energy of resistance welders and induction furnace supplies from the same three-phase source.</dd>
</dl></section>
</article>
